es", "Yes"], ["Caramel Color", "Yes", "Yes", "Yes"], ["Caffeine Content", "Yes", "Higher than regular", "Yes"]]], "code_blocks": [], "meta_description": "Approved by Dr. Robert Cook - The artificial sweeteners aspartame and Ace-K in Pepsi Max are considered safe by health authorities at current exposure levels, despite lingering controversies. Caffeine's effects are dose-dependent and individualized; moderation is essential. Pepsi Max's acidity can erode dental enamel, so dental hygiene is important. Its role in weight gain or appetite is inconclusive, with evidence suggesting both sides. Regular consumption in the broader context of lifestyle factors may influence diabetes risk. Compared with regular soda, Pepsi Max offers fewer calories but similar additives and dental risks.", "meta_keywords": "", "og_image": "https://isitbadforyou.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/article/pictures/1557/big_Is_Pepsi_Max_bad_for_you__1.jpg", "js_rendered": false, "js_message": "", "success": true, "error": "", "truncated": false, "fetched_bytes": 75246, "total_bytes": 75246}}